\subsubsection{Challenge 2: Sanity Checks Leading to Early-Exit Paths}

The second challenge stems from dynamic shape checks in production libraries.
High-performance operators are often developed under strict constraints, such as mandatory memory alignment (e.g., dimensions must be multiples of 32) or equality of dimensions across tensors (e.g., the sequence length of key and value tensors must be equal for the Flash Attention operator).
Consequently, these operators must verify input validity at runtime for robustness.

Consider a simplified Flash Attention computation:
\begin{equation*}
    \text{Attention}(Q, K, V, M) = \text{softmax}\left(\frac{QK^\top}{\sqrt{d_k}} + M\right) V,
\end{equation*}
where $Q \in \mathbb{R}^{B_q \times S_q \times d_k}$, $K \in \mathbb{R}^{B_k \times S_k \times d_k}$, $V \in \mathbb{R}^{B_v \times S_v \times d_v}$, and $M \in \mathbb{R}^{L_q \times L_k}$ (the mask can be broadcast to $(B_q, S_q, S_k)$).
The batch dimensions $B_q$, $B_k$, $B_v$ and sequence lengths $S_q$, $S_k$, $S_v$, $L_q$, $L_k$ are dynamic dimensions that vary across inference requests.
The operator imposes several constraints:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $B_q = B_k = B_v$ (batch sizes must match across query, key, and value tensors);
    \item $S_k = S_v$ (key and value sequence lengths must match);
    \item $S_q = S_k$ for causal attention;
    \item $L_q$ and $L_k$ must be broadcastable to $S_q$ and $S_k$, respectively (i.e., $L_q = S_q$ or $L_q = 1$, and similarly for $L_k$).
\end{itemize}
These constraints are enforced via runtime sanity checks, leading to early-exit paths when violated.

\autoref{lst:early_return_code} illustrates an example where sanity checks create branches that handle errors and exit the function immediately, referred to as an early-exit path.
The function \texttt{set\_t} is a simplified tiling function from a Flash Attention operator, where \texttt{sq} and \texttt{sk} represent the sequence length dimensions of the key and value tensors, respectively.
The Flash Attention operator requires these two dimensions to be equal.
Since both \texttt{sq} and \texttt{sk} are dynamic dimensions, static analysis cannot determine whether the call to \texttt{\@check(\%sq, \%sk)} (line 4 in \autoref{fig:dynamic_shape_check_demo}) returns \texttt{SUCCESS}.
Therefore, the compiler conservatively assumes both the success path (BB1->BB2->BB3) and the early‑exit path (BB1->BB3) are feasible.
This leads to merging of values at BB3, causing the constant 32 stored in \texttt{t} to be polluted and treated as unknown.
Standard path-insensitive data-flow analysis leads to specialization failure because it merges values from both the valid and early-exit paths. In \autoref{fig:dynamic_shape_check_demo}, the value stored at the memory location pointed to by \texttt{\%t} at BB3 is treated as non-constant because the early-exit path (BB1 to BB3) fails to assign a value. 
This prevents the compiler from propagating the constant 32 at line 8. 
While symbolic execution~\cite{Cadar2008KLEEUnassistedAutomatic,Yao2024FalconFusedApproach} can distinguish these paths, it is computationally expensive and suffers from path explosion.

