\textit{Insight: Pattern-Based Early-Exit Path Identification.} 
We observe that dynamic shape operators use canonical patterns to signal failure, primarily through compile-time known status codes (e.g., \texttt{SUCCESS=0}, \texttt{ERROR=-1}) or by throwing exceptions at runtime. 
Following the API contract, output parameters and kernel launches are only valid upon successful check. 
Therefore, values originating from early-exit paths are irrelevant for Kernel Specialization.

For a given function, \mysys identifies control-flow edges that first lead to early exits by back-tracing the root-cause of error status code from the exit block.
the conditional branch statement that cause the program to return an error status code or throw an exception. 
\mysys performs inter-procedural analysis to recognize these failure patterns across function boundaries.
Crucially, this approach involves no risk of incorrect path pruning. 
We only mark a control flow edge as an early-exit edge if it strictly matches the predefined failure patterns.

By marking these edges, \mysys can shield the subsequent forward data-flow analysis from polluted values. This allows the constant propagation pass to focus exclusively on the success paths, which are the only paths where the kernel will actually execute. Consequently, we achieve higher precision than path-insensitive methods without facing the path explosion challenges of symbolic execution.

